"Breaking the Infinite Barrier in the 1/3–2/3 Conjecture (https://arxiv.cnoctave.top/pdf/2609.30888)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; **Conjecture 1.1** (\\(\\frac13\\)-\\(\\frac23\\) conjecture). ... where the probability is over uniform random \\(f\\in\\mathcal{E}(P)\\). ... Date: October 30, 2026. ... * Partially ordered sets, linear extensions, 1/3–2/3 conjecture, balance constants, correlation inequalities, Kahn–Saks conjecture, log-concavity.\nIn this paper we break the infinite barrier by a microscopic margin.\n\n**1.1. Background.** Let \\(P := (X,\\prec)\\) be a finite poset, where \\(X\\) is a finite set of size \\(n := |X|\\), and \\(\\prec\\) is a partial order on \\(X\\). We write \\([n] := \\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\). A *linear extension* of \\(P\\) is a bijection \\(f : X \\to [n]\\) that is order preserving: \\(x \\prec y\\) in \\(P\\) implies \\(f(x)<f(y)\\) for all \\(x,y\\in X\\). Let \\(\\mathcal{E}(P)\\) denote the set of linear extensions of \\(P\\).\n\n**Conjecture 1.1** (\\(\\frac13\\)-\\(\\frac23\\) conjecture). Let \\(P := (X,\\prec)\\) be a finite poset that is not a chain. Then there exist elements \\(x,y\\in X\\), such that\n\\[\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\frac13 \\leq \\mathbb{P}[f(x)<f(y)] \\leq \\frac23,\n\\]\nwhere the probability is over uniform random \\(f\\in\\mathcal{E}(P)\\).\n\nThe conjecture is a central open problem in order theory, independently proposed by Kislitsyn [Kis68] and Fredman [Fre75], see also [Lin84], in connection to sorting with partial information. The simplicity and elegance of the \\(\\frac13\\)-\\(\\frac23\\) conjecture have attracted much attention, and numerous special cases of the conjecture have been established in the literature.\n\nThese known special cases include: width two posets [Lin84], posets with symmetries [GHP87], semiorders [Bri89], posets of height at most \\(c\\log\\log n\\) for a suitable absolute constant \\(c>0\\) [Fri93], 6-thin posets [Pec08], \\(N\\)-free posets [Zag12], Young diagrams [OS18], posets whose cover graph is a forest [Zag19], and posets with at most 14 elements [Gup26]. We refer to [CP25, §13] for an introduction to the problem, additional special cases and further references.\n\nIn a major breakthrough, Kahn and Saks [KS84] used geometric inequalities to show that the inequality (1.1) holds with slightly weaker constants:\n\\[\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\frac{3}{11} \\leq \\mathbb{P}[f(x)<f(y)] \\leq \\frac{8}{11}.\n\\]\n\nDate: October 30, 2026.\n\n2020 *Mathematics Subject Classification.* Primary 06A07; Secondary 05A20, 60C05.\n\n*Key words and phrases.* Partially ordered sets, linear extensions, 1/3–2/3 conjecture, balance constants, correlation inequalities, Kahn–Saks conjecture, log-concavity.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe $1/3$--$2/3$ conjecture · Prove2Me (https://prove2.me/theorems/52aca793-5aeb-48cf-8a58-9226614f4509)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # The $1/3$1/3--$2/3$2/3 conjecture ... by Lucas · Sep 15, 2026 · Mathlib 0df444a (Lean v4.33.1) ...     theorem one_third_two_thirds_conjecture (P : Type) [Fintype P] [PartialOrder P]\n\n# The $1/3$1/3--$2/3$2/3 conjecture\n\nOpen\n\n`FCP.Order.one_third_two_thirds_conjecture`\n\nby Lucas · Sep 15, 2026 · Mathlib 0df444a (Lean v4.33.1)\n\ncombinatorics order\n\nThe $1/3$1/3--$2/3$2/3 conjecture (Kislitsyn; Fredman; Linial). In every finite partially ordered set that is not totally ordered there are two elements $x, y$x,y such that the proportion of linear extensions in which $x$x precedes $y$y lies in $[1/3, 2/3]$[1/3,2/3]. Equivalently, sorting a finite poset by comparisons always admits a nearly balanced query. The best unconditional bound replaces $1/3$1/3 by $(5-\\sqrt5)/10 \\approx 0.276$(5−5​)/10≈0.276 (Brightwell--Felsner--Trotter); the conjecture is known for width-two posets, for semiorders and for several other classes.\n\nPreamble\n    \n    import Mathlib\n    import Definitions.Def_FCP_LinearExtensions\n\nFormal statement\n    \n    namespace FCP.Order\n    \n    theorem one_third_two_thirds_conjecture (P : Type) [Fintype P] [PartialOrder P]\n        (h_not_total : ¬ ∀ x y : P, x ≤ y ∨ y ≤ x) :\n\nWrite $\\mathcal{L}$L for the set of order-preserving bijections $e$e from $P$P onto $\\{0, \\dots, |P|-1\\}${0,…,∣P∣−1}. The conclusion asserts the existence of elements $x, y \\in P$x,y∈P such that the rational number\n\n$$\\frac{\\#\\{e \\in \\mathcal{L} : e(x) < e(y)\\}}{\\#\\mathcal{L}}$$#L#{e∈L:e(x)<e(y)}​\n\nlies in the closed interval $[1/3, 2/3]$[1/3,2/3], where $\\#$# denotes the natural-number cardinality of a set (which is $0$0 for infinite sets, not relevant here).\n\nPoints to note: $x$x and $y$y are not required to be distinct or incomparable; the interval is closed at both endpoints; the counting is over order-preserving bijections onto an initial segment, which is the standard model of linear extensions; and if $\\mathcal{L}$L were empty the quotient would be $0/0 = 0$0/0=0, which is not in $[1/3, 2/3]$[1/3,2/3], so the statement implicitly requires linear extensions to exist (they always do for a finite poset).\n\nHuman review\n\n  * Endorsed by Shuze Chen · Sep 17, 2026\n\n  * Endorsed by Lucas · Sep 17, 2026\n\nConfirmed by the mission captain (proposal self-audit).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Gold Partition Conjecture Holds through Fourteen Elements (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23926)\nciteturn6academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Date: Mon Jul 27 01:41:27 2026 ... It follows, in particular, that the 1/3--2/3 Conjecture holds through order 14, one order beyond the previous complete mutual-rank-probability census.\nTitle: The Gold Partition Conjecture Holds through Fourteen Elements\nAuthors: Anish Gupta\nDate: Mon Jul 27 01:41:27 2026\n\nPeczarski verified the Gold Partition Conjecture for posets with at most 11 elements in 2006. We extend this exhaustive frontier to 14 elements. At order 14, the unique chain is set aside and every one of the remaining 1,338,193,159,770 isomorphism classes receives one of Peczarski's certificates. It follows, in particular, that the 1/3--2/3 Conjecture holds through order 14, one order beyond the previous complete mutual-rank-probability census. The calculation uses exact integer recurrences on the lattice of order ideals and was divided into 4,096 deterministic shards. The complete shard archive, source, and independent small-order checks accompany the paper.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1/3–2/3 conjecture (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1/3%E2%80%932/3_conjecture)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; There is an alternative and equivalent statement of the 1/3–2/3 conjecture in the language of probability theory. ... *  Linial (1984), Theorem 2; Sah (2021). ... *  Aires, Max; Chan, Swee Hong; Pak, Igor; Panova, Greta (2026-09-25), Breaking the Infinite Barrier in the $\\frac{1}{3}$--$\\frac{2}{3}$ Conjecture, arXiv, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2609.30888, arXiv:2609.30888, retrieved 2026-10-11\n1/3–2/3 conjecture\nUnsolved problem on partial orders\nA partial order formed by the series composition of one-element and three-element partial orders. Among its 27 linear extensions, the bottom left element occurs prior to the bottom right element in 9 out of 27. Partial orders with this structure are the only known extreme cases for the 1/3–2/3 conjecture. image\n\nIn order theory, a branch of mathematics, the 1/3–2/3 conjecture states that, if one is comparison sorting a set of items then, no matter what comparisons may have already been performed, it is always possible to choose the next comparison in such a way that it will reduce the number of possible sorted orders by a factor of 2/3 or better. Equivalently, in every finite partially ordered set that is not totally ordered, there exists a pair of elements x and y with the property that at least 1/3 and at most 2/3 of the linear extensions of the partial order place x earlier than y.\nIt was listed as a featured unsolved problem at the founding of the journal Order, and remains unsolved; being called \"one of the most intriguing problems in the combinatorial theory of posets.\"\n\nA survey of the conjecture was produced in 1999.\n## Partial results\n\nThe 1/3–2/3 conjecture is known to be true for certain special classes of partial orders, including partial orders of width two, partial orders of height two, partial orders with at most 13 elements, partial orders in which each element is incomparable to at most six others, series-parallel partial orders, partial orders whose Hasse diagram is N-free, semiorders, and polytrees. A 2026 preprint reports verification through 14 elements. In the limit as n goes to infinity, the proportion of n-element partial orders that obey the 1/3–2/3 conjecture approaches 100%.\n\nIn 1995, Graham Brightwell, Stefan Felsner, and William Trotter proved that, for any finite partial order P that is not total, ${\\textstyle \\delta (P)\\geq 1/2-{\\sqrt {5}}/10\\approx 0.276}$. Their results improve previous weaker bounds of the same type. They use the probabilistic interpretation of ${\\displaystyle \\delta (P)}$ to extend its definition to certain infinite partial orders; in that context, they show that their bounds are optimal, in that there exist infinite partial orders with ${\\textstyle \\delta (P)=1/2-{\\sqrt {5}}/10}$. In 2026, a preprint by Max Aires, Swee Hong Chan, Igor Pak, and Greta Panova reported an improved bound to ${\\displaystyle \\delta (P)\\geq 1/2-{\\sqrt {5}}/10+\\varepsilon }$ for finite posets, where ${\\displaystyle \\varepsilon =3^{-3^{16\\times 10^{18}}}}$. Taken together, these results establish a separation between finite and infinite partial orders in the context of the 1/3-2/3 conjecture.\n## Applications\n\nIn 1984 Jeff Kahn and Saks proposed the following application for the problem: suppose one wishes to comparison sort a totally ordered set X, for which some partial order information is already known in the form of a partial order on X. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDOMES OVER CURVES 17 (https://www.math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/K-curve.pdf)\nciteturn6search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; DOMES OVER CURVES 17 ... Denote by \\(\\chi:\\mathcal{R}^3\\to\\{1,2,3\\}\\) a coloring of \\(\\mathcal{R}^3\\). ... A special case of this, when \\(\\Upsilon\\) is union of two triangles is especially important in view of the *Steinhaus problem*, see §6.5. ... ** *For every integral curve* \\(\\gamma\\in \\mathcal{M}_n\\), *there is a unit rhombus* \\(\\rho\\in \\mathcal{M}_4\\), *and a dome over* \\(\\gamma\\cup\\rho\\).\nWe say that \\([xyz]\\subset \\mathbb{R}^3\\) is a rainbow triangle in \\(\\chi\\), if \\([xyz]\\) is a unit triangle, and \\(\\chi(x)=1,\\ \\chi(y)=2,\\ \\chi(z)=3\\).\n\n**Proposition 5.11.** *Let* \\(\\rho=[uvwx]\\subset \\mathbb{R}^3\\) *be a unit rhombus. Suppose there is a coloring* \\(\\chi:\\mathcal{R}^3\\to\\{1,2,3\\}\\) *with no rainbow triangles, and such that* \\(\\chi(u)=\\chi(v)=1,\\ \\chi(w)=2,\\ \\chi(x)=3\\). *Then* \\(\\rho\\) *cannot be domed.*\n\n*Proof.* By contradiction, suppose \\(S\\) is a 2-dimensional triangulated surface with the boundary \\(\\partial S=\\rho\\). Consider a closed 2-manifold \\(M:=S\\cup [uvx]\\cup [vwx]\\). By *Sperner’s Lemma* for closed 2-manifolds, see [25, Cor. 3.1], the number of rainbow triangles in \\(M\\) is even. Note that triangle \\([vwx]\\) is rainbow, while triangle \\([uvx]\\) is not. Thus \\(S\\) has at least one rainbow triangle, a contradiction. □\n\nThe idea to use the coloring to prove that some curves cannot be domed can be illustrated with the following conjecture:\n\n**Conjecture 5.12.** *Let* \\(\\rho_{\\diamond}=[uvwx]\\subset \\mathbb{R}^3\\) *be a unit rhombus defined above. -----